      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=1$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient.",
      "worked_solution": "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5(1+h)^{2}-5(1)^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5+10h+5{h}^{2}-5}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{10h+5{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(10+5h) \\\\  & =10+(0) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)-f(1)}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-5(1)^{2}}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5({x}^{2}-1)}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}5(x+1) \\\\  & =5(1+1) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
